Z||000000||EMF||04-21-99||07:55 PM||||just out of curiosity, how do you all work<br>on your fiero's, do you just drop the entire cradle out<br>this seems like the easiest way to work on them<br>but how do you get it back in, i just bought 2 fieros<br>and dropped the engine out of the one that is blown up, not to hard but now i need to work on the one that runs<br>and i dont think i can do it, without lots of expensive hoists or lifts
Z||000001||PaUL||04-22-99||03:15 AM||||The first time I took out my engine, I went according to the Haynes manual. Dropped it on a dolly, lifted the body up. Everytime since, Ive used a hoist. Ive found it much easier to lift it out than to drop it.
Z||000002||Carrolles||04-22-99||08:47 AM||||I'm planning a clutch job on mine soon and have been asking around for the best way to drop the drivetrain.  V8-Archie has instructions on his web site that sound pretty good.  You jack up the car so the inside top of the right rear wheelwell is about 48"off the floor.  Support the car on jack stands just forward of the rear wheel wells.  The front wheels should be about 6" off the ground before you start also or else you will jam the front air dam into the concrete.  You then use an engine hoise (cherry pickers cost around $200 or can be rented pretty cheap) to lower the cradle with engine and trans down onto a dolly. Then it rolls out of the right rear wheel well.<br>I believe this gives more control than trying to raise the car off and back onto the cradle.<br>By the way I've seen Fieros lifted from the trunk hold down latch. It is very strong. Jack Ditter used this method to raise his car to install his Northstar.<br>Good luck,<br>Carroll
Z||000003||EMF||04-22-99||05:07 PM||||i thought of the trunk latch thing<br>but wasnt sure if it would hold. <br>i need to replace my engine cradle, pronto<br>looks like im going to just have to fudge it. <br>i have some kind of homemade hoist in my garage. <br>i wish i could afford to have some kind of professional do it, but oh well.<br>thanks for the trunk latch idea. 
Z||000004||EMF||04-22-99||05:11 PM||||oh yea, i took the entire rear end out of <br>an 86 four banger with a blbown engine all by myself, and then <br>had 2 guys help me pick the car up and move <br>it over the engine and drop it on the ground<br>it works,  but we broke lots of shit<br>not very professional, fiero's dont wiegh all <br>that much once you get rid of the engine
Z||000005||Cliff Pennock||04-22-99||05:48 PM||||Well, space is limited here in Holland (largest population density in the world!) so are houses with a garage. If I want to work on my car I'll have to do it on the street. That together with the fact it rains here 300 out of 365 days a year, doesn't make working on my Fiero one of the most pleasant things to do...
Z||000006||ezsurf||04-22-99||07:50 PM||||Paul,<br>I'm getting ready to pull my engine this weekend.  I have the Pontiac dealer service manual.  The book shows they dropped it out the bottom.  I was planning on doing it this way, since I do not have a hoist.  This procedure says to remove the brake calipers and secure them out of the way, and remove the strut bolts and mark the struts for realignment.  I really don't want to unbolt the struts.  Is this absolutely necessary if you remove the engine from underneath?  If it's 10 times easier pulling it from the top, I'll rent a hoist.  If this is the case, I would like to know where you attached the lift chain to the top of the engine to pull it.  This question goes out to anyone who has a good amount of experience removing this engine.
Z||000007||Pretender||04-22-99||08:10 PM||||on the street and in the grass...i know your pain!..:o)
Z||000008||EMF||04-23-99||11:32 AM||||ezsurf: i dont have a good amount of experience removinig engines, but there should be 2 things that stick up off the top of the engine to hook the chains, one just to the right of the oil filler cap, and in front of your thermostat housing.<br>i am also planning on removinig my engine as soon as it stops raining, and all i have is alot of  wooden blocks, a  floor jack, bottle jack, and a steel frame i can use hold the engine in  place while i  change  the cradle
Z||000009||EMF||04-23-99||11:34 AM||||oh yea, if  anyone has a game plan for replacinig the engine cradle without actually taking the engine out and off the old cradle let me know.
Z||000010||PaUL||04-23-99||01:50 PM||||When I lifted my engine, I bolted the chain to the heads. One end of the chain bolted to<br>where the AC bracket is, the other to the opposite side of the other head. <p>Ive found lifting is much easier than dropping, mainly because your not working under the car as much. This saves on the back pain and putting that cradle back in right is not easy either. I had to take the battery and tray out because it was in the way. I also had to remove the decklid. I dont have an AC compressor but I think this is the main reason for dropping instead of lifting. <p>As for dropping just the cradle, there is a proscribed method. GM makes a special bar that attaches between the strut towers. This is used to temporarily suspend the engine while the cradle is dropped. 
Z||000011||ezsurf||04-24-99||10:02 PM||||PaUL,<br>I haven't managed to get the engine out of the car yet.  The book procedure removes it from underneath.  I am trying to "take it from the top."  This car has a 2.8l V-6 with a 4 speed manual transmission.  I don't know if your car has a manual transmission, but I am still trying to figure out how to seperate the axels from the transmission.  If you remove the engine from the bottom, it looks like the axels come with it. But, you can't take it out from the top that way.  I found lift brackets attached to the engine, lift points are no longer a problem.  I would like to know what engine/transmission set up you are dealing with.  The top removal may not be compatible with my engine/transmission set up.
Z||000012||theogre||04-25-99||09:47 PM||||the axils come out same way as for axil repair. you diassemble the wheel end, then they pull out of the tranny. it's time consuming but not hard.
Z||000013||PaUL||04-26-99||02:10 PM||||If youve removed the rear axels and hub assembly as much times as I have, then its pretty fast. Youll need a large socket to fit the nut at the end of each axel shaft. Remove this then pull the hub with a puller. Then separate the ball joint from the knuckle by removing the bolt and nut that holds it in place. Stand on the end of the Aarm and put your weight on it to get it free. Now the end of the axel shaft should be in the middle of the knuckle without the hub. Youll have to pull the knuckle/strut tower away from the car and push the shaft down to get it free from the knuckle. The shaft should then pull right out from the tranny. Put a pan under the tranny before you do this because alot of oil will drain out from the axel shaft hole. <p>When I pull the engine from the top, I just pull the engine, not both engine and trans. But you should be able to do both. 
Z||000014||ezsurf||04-26-99||08:51 PM||||PaUL,<br>I like the idea of pulling the engine alone.  As I have mentioned, I am dealing with the V-6. I don't know which engine you have.  Is there enough room to seperate the engine from the transmission and still remove it topside?  Also, how difficult is it to unbolt the transmission?  That is, can you get to all of the bolts easy enough, or is there that one that you just can't get to?
Z||000015||Howard||04-27-99||10:54 AM||||i have a 86 with a v6 and just rebuilt the<br>motor last fall, and did it from the top side<br>with no problem.
Z||000016||PaUL||04-27-99||03:29 PM||||the bolts are easy to get to. a few of them may have to be removed from the bottom. You have to gently pry the engine from the trans.
